## Project Structure (Domain-Driven Design)

```
src/main/java/com/example/
├── {domain}/                    # One package per domain
│   ├── domain/                  # Domain layer - entities and repository interfaces
│   │   ├── {Entity}.java        # JPA entity
│   │   └── {Entity}Repository.java  # Spring Data JPA repository interface
│   ├── application/             # Application layer - business logic
│   │   └── {Entity}ApplicationService.java
│   └── interfaces/              # Interface layer - REST controllers
│       └── rest/
│           ├── {Entity}Controller.java
│           └── {Entity}Mapper.java
├── shared/                      # Cross-cutting concerns
│   ├── application/
│   ├── domain/
│   └── interfaces/rest/
└── api/generated/               # OpenAPI generated code (do not edit)
```

## Transaction Management

### Use @Transactional for Read‑Write Operations
The most common approach is to annotate service methods with `@Transactional` for atomicity:

```java
// Good – clear transactional boundaries
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class EntityApplicationService {
    private final EntityRepository entityRepository;
    private final EntityMapper entityMapper;

    @Transactional
    public EntityDto updateEntity(String ref, UpdateEntityRequestDto request) {
        Entity entity = entityRepository.findByPublicId(ref)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new EntityNotFoundException("Entity", ref));

        if (request.getTitle() != null) {
            entity.setTitle(request.getTitle());
        }
        // ... other field updates

        return entityMapper.toDto(entity);  // save happens automatically on flush
    }
}
```

### Use TransactionTemplate Only When You Need Explicit Control
For most cases `@Transactional` is sufficient. Use `TransactionTemplate` only when you need to programmatically manage transaction boundaries inside a single method (rare).

### For Create Operations, Use a Separate @Transactional Method
If sequence number generation must be atomic with the insert, put both in a dedicated `@Transactional` helper method:

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class EntityApplicationService {
    private final EntityRepository entityRepository;
    private final EntityMapper entityMapper;

    @Transactional
    public EntityDto createEntity(CreateEntityRequestDto request) {
        int sequenceNumber = getNextSequenceNumber();  // read inside transaction
        Entity entity = new Entity(request.getRef(), sequenceNumber, ...);
        entity = entityRepository.save(entity);
        return entityMapper.toDto(entity);
    }

    private int getNextSequenceNumber() {
        // ... fetch from database
    }
}
```

### Don’t Split Transaction Scopes Without Reason
Splitting the read and write into separate transactions (e.g., using `TransactionTemplate` for the save only) can lead to inconsistency if the data changes between the read and write. In most enterprise applications, the overhead of a slightly longer transaction is negligible compared to the correctness gained.

## Lombok Usage

### Use @RequiredArgsConstructor for Dependency Injection
Never write constructors manually for Spring beans:

```java
// Good
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class EntityApplicationService {
    private final EntityRepository entityRepository;
    private final EntityMapper entityMapper;
}

// Bad
@Service
public class EntityApplicationService {
    private final EntityRepository entityRepository;

    public EntityApplicationService(EntityRepository entityRepository) {
        this.entityRepository = entityRepository;
    }
}
```

